snmptrapd mirror — central trap receiver with per-site forwarding

Instead of one snmptrapd per site receiving directly on UDP/162 (possible
port conflicts when two sites run at once), a single central snmptrapd
listens on UDP/162 and forwards every trap to the enabled sites' own
snmptrapd ports (127.0.0.1:<site port>). The per-site trap log then carries
the original sender IP (addForwarderInfo marker), not the mirror's own.
The central component is owned by snmptrapd_mirror_watch (systemd): it
discovers enabled sites (CONFIG_SNMPTRAPD='on'), maintains the managed
section of /etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf and keeps the mirror running.
Enabling trap receiving in a site (omd config set SNMPTRAPD on) is the
only operator action needed — the mirror converges within ~60 s.

1) prerequisite (once)
apt-get update && apt-get install -y snmptrapd
   # /usr/sbin/snmptrapd (package "snmptrapd"; "snmp" adds the client tools
   # used to send test traps). Net-SNMP 5.9+ required (addForwarderInfo).
   # If the distribution enabled the stock snmptrapd socket unit, it binds
   # UDP/162 as well — remove it so only the mirror owns the port:
   systemctl disable --now snmptrapd.socket 2>/dev/null || true

2) as root (installation; the OMD package already placed the files)
   /opt/omd/versions/default/bin/:            trap_targets, snmptrapd_mirror_watch
                                              (updated: snmptrap_logger.sh,
                                               samplicate_watch)
   /opt/omd/versions/default/share/snmptrapd_mirror/:
                                                snmptrapd_mirror.service,
                                                snmptrapd_mirror_watch.service,
                                                snmptrapd.conf.example, README.snmptrapd
/bin/cp /opt/omd/versions/default/share/snmptrapd_mirror/*.service /etc/systemd/system
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable snmptrapd_mirror_watch
systemctl start snmptrapd_mirror_watch
   # the mirror (snmptrapd_mirror) is started/stopped by the watcher
   # automatically; enabling it is optional (boot ordering is covered by
   # Requires=snmptrapd_mirror_watch.service). The mirror unit runs
   # /usr/sbin/snmptrapd directly under systemd — before the watcher's
   # first cycle it may run with an absent/empty configuration for a few
   # seconds; that is harmless and converges within one cycle.
   # Note: install EITHER this mirror method OR the legacy samplicate
   # method. Running both (or leaving any other owner on UDP/162) is not
   # reconciled automatically — the second bind fails in the unit journal
   # (journalctl -u snmptrapd_mirror); clean up your own setup.

3) as site-user (enable trap receiving where wanted)
omd config set SNMPTRAPD on
omd restart snmptrapd

Configuration ownership
   /etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf — the watcher recreates it from
   snmptrapd.conf.example when it is missing. Everything between the
   marker lines
       ### BEGIN snmptrapd_mirror (managed by snmptrapd_mirror_watch — do not edit) ###
       ### END snmptrapd_mirror ###
   is generated (disableAuthorization yes, doNotLogTraps 1, addForwarderInfo
   yes, doNotFork yes, per-site '# site <name>' + forward lines); manual
   edits there are reset on the next cycle (SECTION-RESET log line). Lines
   outside the markers are operator-owned; changes there are picked up by a
   gap-free SIGHUP reload (APPLY log line). An invalid file (missing
   addForwarderInfo, doNotFork, disableAuthorization or doNotLogTraps — or
   doNotLogTraps set to something other than the number 1) is never applied
   (REJECTED log line names the problem). The mirror carries NO
   authCommunity lines and forwards every trap regardless of its community;
   which communities a site accepts is decided in that site's own
   /omd/sites/<site>/etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf (operator-owned; e.g. add
   'authCommunity log,execute,net <community>' for a custom community, then
   'omd reload <site>'). Journald carries OPERATIONAL STATE ONLY — via
   syslog (tag snmptrapd, facility daemon: startup line, MIB-parse noise,
   reload marker, stop, forward errors; journalctl -t snmptrapd) and via
   the mirror unit's stderr (journalctl -u snmptrapd_mirror). It
   deliberately does NOT contain one line per trap: the daemon runs without
   a -L destination and the generated 'doNotLogTraps 1' gates the remaining
   default syslog handler, so thousands of traps cannot flood the journal.
   The record of the traps is the per-site log
   /omd/sites/<site>/var/log/snmp/traps.log.

Operations
   trap_targets check            # one-glance sync status (exit 0)
   trap_targets method           # none | snmptrapd_mirror | samplicate
   journalctl -t snmptrapd_mirror_watch -f      # watcher decisions (D1-D7)
   journalctl -t snmptrapd                         # the mirror's operational
                                                    # log (syslog, facility
                                                    # daemon — NOT one line
                                                    # per trap)
   journalctl -u snmptrapd_mirror                  # same operational state via
                                                    # the daemon's stderr
   ss -ulnp | grep ':162 '                     # who owns UDP/162

Troubleshooting (watcher log lines and what they mean)
   START/STOP/UPDATE/SECTION-RESET/APPLY/CONFIG-CREATED: normal lifecycle lines.
   REJECTED: /etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf invalid — ...
       the file failed validation; the previous configuration stays
       effective until the file is fixed (fix or delete the file).
   NOTE: snmptrapd_mirror did not come up after section update (see: journalctl -u snmptrapd_mirror)
       systemd could not start the mirror — the usual cause is another
       process owning UDP/162 (a leftover legacy samplicate, a mis-enabled
       distro snmptrapd.socket, or a second listener). This is not
       reconciled automatically: stop/remove the other owner; the watcher
       starts the mirror within one cycle. Also check that the prerequisite
       daemon is installed (apt-get install -y snmptrapd).
   "Did the mirror see this trap?"
       journald does NOT log individual traps by design (journal volume at
       production trap rates). Check the per-site trap log
       /omd/sites/<site>/var/log/snmp/traps.log — a forwarded trap lands
       there with its original sender IP. If the trap is missing everywhere,
       verify that the mirror owns UDP/162 (ss -ulnp | grep ':162 ') and
       that the community is accepted at the site: look for an
       'authCommunity log,execute,net <community>' line in the site's own
       /omd/sites/<site>/etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf and reload the site
       (omd reload <site>) after changing it.

Rollback
systemctl stop snmptrapd_mirror_watch snmptrapd_mirror
rm /etc/systemd/system/snmptrapd_mirror_watch.service /etc/systemd/system/snmptrapd_mirror.service
systemctl daemon-reload
rm /etc/snmp/snmptrapd.conf
# restore .pre-feature/ snmptrap_logger.sh and samplicate_watch if the
# legacy behavior is required
